(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-pdm-exam-2-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What distinguishes a transthoracic from a transesophageal echocardiogram?","choices":["One uses Doppler and the other does not","One uses ionizing radiation while the other does not","The transducer is outside the body in one, inside in the other","One assesses valves and the other assesses chambers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and everything else about the two -- invasiveness, sedation, contraindications, and how much posterior detail you get -- follows from that single difference. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which of these is one of the described contiguous groups?","choices":["Leads I, II and III","Leads II, III and aVF","Leads aVR, aVL and aVF","Leads V1, V3 and V5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The other two groups are V1 to V4, and leads I, aVL, V5 and V6. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has a platelet count of 6,000 per microliter (adult reference 140,000 to 400,000). Which risk is highest?","choices":["Spontaneous intracranial bleeding","Bruising only with major trauma","Excess bleeding only with surgery","No added risk until the count is lower still"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Below 10,000 per microliter the patient is at risk for spontaneous intracranial bleeding and serious hemorrhage. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A strip has no three-second markers. How many large boxes make up a six-second strip?","choices":["Thirty large boxes","Fifteen large boxes","Five large boxes","Sixty large boxes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each large box is 0.2 seconds, so fifteen large boxes make 3 seconds and thirty make 6 seconds. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a low platelet count with a normal prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and platelet function analysis. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Immune thrombocytopenia","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","von Willebrand disease","Early liver failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immune thrombocytopenia, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic uremic syndrome and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia all give a low count with normal clotting times. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man is found to have a markedly elevated lipoprotein(a) after a premature myocardial infarction. Whom should testing be extended to?","choices":["No one; it is not inherited","Only his spouse","His first-degree relatives","Only relatives over 65"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Testing is indicated in those with a personal or family history of atherosclerotic disease, with cascade testing in appropriate families, because the level is more than 90% genetically determined. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which of these is a stated indication for exercise stress testing?","choices":["Cardiac assessment before non-cardiac surgery in a high-risk patient","Acute chest pain before acute coronary syndrome has been excluded","Suspected aortic dissection","Screening an asymptomatic patient with no risk factors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside symptoms suggesting ischemia, known ischemic disease with a change in status, prior revascularization and newly diagnosed heart failure. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A six-second strip contains eight QRS complexes. What is the ventricular rate?","choices":["Forty-eight beats per minute","Eighty beats per minute","Eight beats per minute","One hundred and sixty beats per minute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The six-second method multiplies the count by ten, since six seconds is a tenth of a minute. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"In which direction does conduction truly travel through the heart?","choices":["Right and up, toward the shoulder","Left and down, toward the anterior chest","Straight downward, in the coronal plane only","Posteriorly, away from the chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and this is why lead II sees the beat so well: it looks along that axis. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 71-year-old has troponin monitored after coronary artery bypass grafting. Which rise defines a surgery-related (type 5) infarction?","choices":["More than 5 times the 99th percentile limit","Any value above the 99th percentile limit","More than 2 times the 99th percentile limit","More than 10 times the 99th percentile limit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Coronary artery bypass graft-related infarction, type 5, needs more than 10 times the 99th percentile upper reference limit, a higher bar than for percutaneous intervention. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How does a vascular ultrasound image form?","choices":["Ionizing radiation is absorbed differently by tissues of differing density","Quartz crystals emit pulses that reflect off tissue","A magnetic field aligns protons which then emit radiofrequency signal","An injected tracer is taken up and emits gamma rays"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The returning echoes are what the machine turns into a picture, so anything that stops sound returning leaves a gap in it. The pulses are high frequency, and what returns depends on the density of what they meet. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has ischemic chest pain. Which troponin result supports acute myocardial infarction?","choices":["A value above the 50th percentile of healthy adults","Any detectable troponin at all on a sensitive assay","A value above the 99th percentile upper reference limit","A value above the 95th percentile of hospital patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Under the Fourth Universal Definition, infarction is evidence of myocardial injury, troponin I or T above the 99th percentile upper reference limit, in the appropriate setting of ischemic symptoms. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In most leads, how far from the baseline may a normal J point sit?","choices":["Within 3 millimeters","Within 5 millimeters, one large box","Within 1 millimeter","Exactly on the baseline, with no allowance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The J point, where the QRS complex ends and the ST segment begins, should be at the baseline, with one millimeter of variance allowed. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a prolonged partial thromboplastin time that does not correct on mixing, and the abnormality depends on phospholipid. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["A specific factor inhibitor","Hemophilia A","Lupus anticoagulant","Hypofibrinogenemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Failure to correct with phospholipid dependence, shown by the dilute Russell viper venom time, is lupus anticoagulant. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has a prolonged partial thromboplastin time that corrects when mixed one to one with normal plasma. What does this suggest?","choices":["An inhibitor","Lupus anticoagulant","A factor deficiency","A platelet disorder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Correction on a mixing study suggests a factor deficiency, because normal plasma supplies the missing factor. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has a bedside bleeding time after a standardized forearm puncture. What is being timed?","choices":["How long plasma takes to clot with tissue factor","How long plasma takes to clot with a phospholipid activator","How long the puncture keeps bleeding","How long a formed clot takes to dissolve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding time is the time in minutes for a standardized, superficial skin puncture to stop bleeding, and it is performed at the bedside. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which complications of coronary angiography occur at the catheter insertion site?","choices":["Contrast nephropathy and arrhythmia","Hematoma and pseudoaneurysm","Esophageal perforation","Pneumothorax"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the pseudoaneurysm is the one to check for deliberately, since it maintains a continuous communication with the artery. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man started a statin two months ago and now has diffuse muscle aches. Which biomarker is the appropriate test?","choices":["Creatine kinase","High-sensitivity troponin","B-type natriuretic peptide","High-sensitivity C-reactive protein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Statin myopathy and rhabdomyolysis are skeletal muscle questions, and creatine kinase is the right test for them. Troponin is not. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Tracer uptake in a region is normal at rest but reduced after stress. What does that pattern indicate?","choices":["Inducible ischemia, from a coronary stenosis","Infarcted, non-viable tissue","Normal myocardium","A pericardial effusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The tissue is alive but cannot increase its supply when demand rises, which is what a stenosis does. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 49-year-old has triglycerides of 180 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL) and no other lipid abnormality. How is this triglyceride level classed?","choices":["A risk enhancer for atherosclerotic disease","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Normal, with no significance","Diagnostic of familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Triglycerides of 150 mg/dL or above mean increased atherosclerotic risk and act as a risk enhancer; severe hypertriglyceridemia begins at 500 mg/dL. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 37-year-old with no symptoms asks whether she needs a lipoprotein(a) level. What does the current guidance say?","choices":["Measure it only after a heart attack","Measure it at least once in all adults","Measure it every year from age 40","Never measure it; it is untreatable"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Current multisociety guidance recommends measuring lipoprotein(a) at least once in all adults for atherosclerotic risk assessment. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What does the R wave represent?","choices":["Depolarization of the anterior left ventricle","Depolarization of the interventricular septum","Depolarization of the lateral wall of the left ventricle","Depolarization of the atria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is the first positive deflection, following either the PR interval or the Q wave. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the normal corrected QT interval range in a man?","choices":["360 to 460 milliseconds","450 to 500 milliseconds","400 to 500 milliseconds","350 to 450 milliseconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The corrected QT interval, which adjusts for heart rate, is normally 350 to 450 milliseconds in men; the female range is slightly higher, at 360 to 460 milliseconds. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 58-year-old's 10-year PREVENT (Predicting Risk of cardiovascular disease EVENTs) estimate is 7%, in the intermediate tier of 5% to under 10%, and she is unsure about starting therapy. What may be considered to reclassify her risk?","choices":["A high-sensitivity troponin","A B-type natriuretic peptide","A coronary artery calcium score","A creatine kinase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the Calculate, Personalize, Reclassify framework, when there is clinical or patient uncertainty at borderline or intermediate risk, a coronary artery calcium score is considered to revise the estimate. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which history contraindicates a transesophageal echocardiogram?","choices":["Cerebral ischemia","A prosthetic heart valve","Odynophagia or dysphagia","Aortic dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A history of painful or difficult swallowing is a contraindication, since a probe must be passed down the esophagus and swallowed to advance. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman's screening panel shows total cholesterol 178 mg/dL (desirable below 200), triglycerides 90 mg/dL (desirable below 150) and HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) 62 mg/dL (higher is favorable). How is it interpreted?","choices":["Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Low HDL-C needing treatment","Suspected familial hypercholesterolemia","A favorable profile"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Total cholesterol and triglycerides are below their desirable limits and HDL-C is high, which is generally favorable. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the R to R interval used to assess?","choices":["Rate, regularity","Ventricular repolarization","Atrial depolarization","The isoelectric baseline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One measurement doing three jobs, which is why comparing consecutive R to R intervals is the first thing done to a strip. Heart blocks. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which of these can an electrocardiogram NOT tell you?","choices":["Whether there is electrical activity in the heart","Whether the patient has a pulse","Where the impulse originates","Whether there are conduction problems"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with hemodynamic status and cardiac output. It shows electrical activity, and electrical activity without a pulse is a recognized and lethal state. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with no bleeding history has a prolonged partial thromboplastin time found before surgery, and a normal prothrombin time. Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor XII","Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor XI"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Factor XII deficiency prolongs the activated partial thromboplastin time but does not cause bleeding. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 59-year-old's troponin is being interpreted. Which troponin subunit has cardiac specificity?","choices":["Troponin C, the calcium-binding component","Troponin I, the inhibitory component","Troponin T, found only in cardiac muscle","None of the three subunits is cardiac"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Troponin I has cardiac specificity. Troponin T is primarily cardiac, but trace amounts are detected in skeletal muscle. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has mucosal bleeding and a normal platelet count. Which studies evaluate von Willebrand disease?","choices":["Prothrombin time and international normalized ratio","D-dimer and fibrin monomers","von Willebrand antigen, activity and factor VIII","Fibrinogen and thrombin time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The von Willebrand studies are von Willebrand factor antigen and activity plus a factor VIII level. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why must a Zenker's diverticulum be identified before a transesophageal study?","choices":["It prevents adequate sedation","It causes a false positive for vegetations","The probe risks entering and perforating the pouch","It makes the patient unable to swallow the local anesthetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and because it is a pouch rather than a narrowing, the operator may feel nothing wrong until the damage is done. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has a D-dimer above the reference limit of 250 micrograms per liter. What does a positive result confirm?","choices":["A deep vein thrombosis specifically","Thrombin and plasmin were both generated","Platelet dysfunction","A factor VIII deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is produced only by plasmin acting on cross-linked fibrin, so its presence confirms that both thrombin generation and plasmin generation have occurred. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 52-year-old with active rheumatoid arthritis has an hs-CRP (high-sensitivity C-reactive protein) drawn for heart risk. What limits its interpretation?","choices":["It reflects only acute plaque rupture","It is not specific to cardiac disease","It is only valid in patients over 75","It needs a fasting sample to be valid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is confounded by any inflammatory or infectious state, and autoimmune disease raises it, so it is not a stand-alone test. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What view of the heart do the precordial leads give?","choices":["A frontal view","A sagittal view only","No spatial view; they measure rate only","A horizontal view"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which complements the frontal plane the limb leads provide, so the two sets together look at the heart from two directions. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 56-year-old's LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol) is at goal, but his triglycerides stay above the desirable level of 150 mg/dL. What do his triglyceride-rich lipoproteins contribute?","choices":["Protection from atherosclerosis","No risk once LDL-C is at goal","A falsely low apoB level","Residual atherosclerotic risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Remnant cholesterol and triglyceride-rich lipoproteins contribute residual risk; VLDL (very low-density lipoprotein) remnants and IDL (intermediate-density lipoprotein) are atherogenic. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the standing of chest radiography in cardiac evaluation?","choices":["It is the primary modality for assessing cardiac function","It has no role in cardiac assessment","It replaces echocardiography when the patient cannot lie flat","It is not the primary modality, but it gives useful clues when obtained"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is rarely the study you order to answer a cardiac question, and frequently the study you already have, which is why reading it well matters. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A corrected QT of 520 milliseconds is reported. How should that be read?","choices":["Normal","Borderline prolongation","Shortened","High risk prolongation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beyond five hundred milliseconds is the high risk band, above the borderline range that begins in the low four hundreds. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a platelet count of 80,000 per microliter (adult reference 140,000 to 400,000). Which term applies?","choices":["Thrombocytosis","A normal count","Pseudothrombocytosis","Thrombocytopenia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A platelet count well below the lower reference limit is thrombocytopenia, here a count under even the 100,000 per microliter figure used to define it. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which structure forms the upper part of the right heart border on a posteroanterior chest radiograph?","choices":["Superior vena cava","Aortic knuckle","Main pulmonary artery segment","Left atrial appendage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It sits above the right atrium along the right border, whereas the aortic knuckle, pulmonary artery segment, left atrial appendage and left ventricle all lie along the left. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has a normal prothrombin time, a prolonged partial thromboplastin time and a prolonged thrombin time. Which drug fits?","choices":["Warfarin","Heparin","Aspirin","Rivaroxaban"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heparin, like dabigatran, prolongs the activated partial thromboplastin time and the thrombin time while leaving the prothrombin time normal. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 45-year-old with a high HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) asks what protein defines that particle. Which is it?","choices":["ApoB-100 (apolipoprotein B-100)","ApoB-48 (apolipoprotein B-48)","Apo(a) (apolipoprotein(a))","ApoA-I (apolipoprotein A-I)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ApoA-I is the major structural protein of HDL, synthesized by the liver and intestines and found on all HDL particles. ApoA-II is second, on about two-thirds of them. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A six-second rhythm strip shows twelve P waves and eleven QRS complexes. What is the atrial rate?","choices":["110 beats per minute","23 beats per minute","12 beats per minute","120 beats per minute"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The atrial rate comes from counting P waves in six seconds and multiplying by ten, so twelve P waves give 120. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man takes warfarin, and his international normalized ratio is 1.4 (goal 2.0 to 3.0). What is the concern?","choices":["Bleeding risk","Clot risk","Platelet failure","Liver failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. On warfarin a low international normalized ratio means too little anticoagulation and a risk of clotting. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"An emergency department switches a 68-year-old's workup to high-sensitivity troponin. What is the main advantage?","choices":["It detects infarction earlier","Any measurable level confirms infarction","It replaces the electrocardiogram","It also measures wall stretch"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. As much as 90 minutes earlier than conventional troponin I and T, which allows a faster rule-out or rule-in and earlier treatment. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does a lead record when the overall vector is net zero?","choices":["A negative deflection","A positive deflection","An isoelectric line","Electrical noise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A flat trace is a finding rather than an absence of one: it says the forces canceled in that lead's direction. With no deflection. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is a recognized limitation of cardiovascular magnetic resonance?","choices":["Interference from lung and bone","Lengthy acquisition time","Exposure to ionizing radiation","Dependence on iodinated contrast"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Along with claustrophobia, a distorted electrocardiogram and the need for electrocardiographic and respiratory gating, the long scan is one of its recognized drawbacks. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does electrophysiological testing do?","choices":["Assesses myocardial perfusion under stress conditions","Measures the cardiothoracic ratio","Visualizes coronary anatomy","Investigates rhythm disorders and locates an arrhythmia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and where applicable it treats as well as diagnoses, by ablating the tissue it has just located. It reveals the precise location, and where applicable treats it by ablation in the same sitting. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Why are the augmented leads considered unipolar?","choices":["They measure between two physical electrodes","They are placed on the chest rather than the limbs","Only one physical electrode is used","They record no voltage difference at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The machine constructs the other half of the comparison, so there is only one real point of view involved. The rest being created by the machine. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How does an exercise stress test detect myocardial ischemia?","choices":["Directly, by imaging myocardial perfusion during exercise","By measuring coronary artery calcium before and after exercise","By measuring ejection fraction at peak exercise","Indirectly, by continuous electrocardiographic monitoring"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It never images the heart at all; it infers ischemia from what the electrocardiogram does as demand rises, which is why the word is indirectly. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is a non-contrast cardiac computed tomography particularly useful for?","choices":["Assessing coronary stenosis","Measuring ejection fraction","Detecting valve vegetations","Coronary artery calcium scoring"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Calcium is dense enough to be seen without contrast, so the study that needs no contrast is the one that measures it. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 58-year-old's high-sensitivity C-reactive protein is repeatedly above the high-risk tier. What does a persistently high level independently predict?","choices":["Heart failure and cardiac mortality","An acute infarction within hours","Skeletal muscle breakdown","A falsely low troponin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High levels are an independent risk factor for heart failure and cardiac mortality, and the level is directly proportional to cardiovascular risk. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman bleeds easily. Her platelet count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are all normal. Which should be investigated next?","choices":["Intrinsic pathway factors","Extrinsic pathway factors","Common pathway factors","Platelet function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normal prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with bleeding suggest platelet dysfunction, so platelet function testing and von Willebrand studies come next. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the J point?","choices":["The peak of the T wave","The start of the P wave","The junction where the QRS complex ends and the ST segment begins","The end of the T wave"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it should sit at the baseline, with about a millimeter of variance tolerated. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How does nuclear perfusion imaging compare with standard and echocardiographic stress testing?","choices":["Its sensitivity and specificity are superior","It is less accurate but faster","It is equivalent in accuracy but avoids radiation","It assesses anatomy rather than perfusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is worth the tracer and the radiation when the simpler tests have not settled the question. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a clot that formed inside his femoral vein and partly blocks flow. Which term names a clot formed in situ within the vascular system?","choices":["Thrombus","Platelet plug","Fibrin degradation product","D-dimer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A thrombus is a blood clot formed in situ within the vascular system of the body that impedes blood flow. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which cardiomyopathy questions is cardiovascular magnetic resonance used to answer?","choices":["The degree of coronary stenosis","Exercise capacity","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Left atrial appendage thrombus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each turns on what the muscle itself is doing, which is where it outperforms the studies that show the arteries. Ischemic against nonischemic, acute myocarditis and cardiac sarcoidosis. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man undergoes a bedside bleeding time (reference 3 to 10 minutes). At which platelet count is the result meaningful?","choices":["Below 50,000 per microliter","Above 100,000 per microliter","Below 20,000 per microliter","Below 10,000 per microliter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding time is only useful if the platelet count is above 100,000 per microliter, because thrombocytopenia itself lengthens bleeding time. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 10-year-old with no family history comes for a well-child visit. Is a lipid screen indicated?","choices":["No, screening starts at age 19","Yes, at ages 9 to 11","No, children are never screened","Only if she is already obese"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Childhood screening at ages 9 to 11 identifies familial hypercholesterolemia and other lipid disorders; lipids are screened again at 19. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man cuts his finger and a platelet plug forms at the injury within minutes. Which term names this step?","choices":["Secondary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Thrombus formation","Primary hemostasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary hemostasis is the formation of a platelet plug at the site of vascular injury; von Willebrand factor and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa carry it out. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
